<TopicPage slug="subjects/dance/foundational/performance-technique-and-expression">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Dance performance is the disciplined union of two different achievements: executing
    movement accurately (physical and technical skill) and making it mean something
    (expressive skill). A dancer can have perfect technique and be invisible on stage —
    lines clean, turns exact, and the audience checking their programmes — because
    technique is what the dancer does, while performance is what the audience
    <em>receives</em>. The reverse failure exists too: expressive performers with weak
    technique whose intentions cannot be read because the body cannot execute them. The
    craft of performance is holding both at once, and the profession's training divides
    its hours exactly accordingly: class for the chassis, rehearsal for the speech.
  </p>
  <p>
    A visitor should also know that dance performance is an examinable discipline with a
    curriculum, not just an apprenticeship: syllabuses name the physical skills,
    the technical vocabulary, the expressive skills, and safe dance practice — the
    anatomy and physiology that make a dancing life sustainable. That last item is the
    one most often ignored at a dancer's peril: dance is an athletic career with an
    artistic calendar, and its injuries cluster precisely where anatomy is ignored.
  </p>
  <p>
    This chapter takes the five layers in order — physical, technical, expressive,
    safe practice, and the mental-rehearsal layer that assembles them under lights.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the five layers of performance" widget="equationStep" config={perfCfg} />

  <h2>Physical and technical skills</h2>
  <p>
    The physical layer is the trainable chassis, and it decomposes into named components
    that can each be trained and measured: <em>strength</em> (especially the deep
    core and the stabilisers of hip and ankle), <em>flexibility</em> (active as much as
    passive — a leg you can hold high matters more than one you can stretch high),
    <em>stamina</em> (muscular endurance across a show's arc), <em>coordination</em>
    (limbs, timing, both sides of the body), and <em>balance</em> (proprioception —
    the body's sense of where it is — trained by standing work on one leg, on half-
    point, on unstable surfaces). The professional insight is that these are
    <em>separate</em>: a flexible dancer without strength is fragile, a strong dancer
    without stamina fades in act two, and every component is trained on purpose rather
    than assumed from youth.
  </p>
  <p>
    Technical skill is a different layer: accuracy within a style's grammar. "Good
    technique" means something different in ballet (turnout from the hips, pointed
    lines, verticality, turn-out en pointe), in contemporary (release of weight,
    floorwork, contraction, weight-sharing with partners), and in street styles (groove,
    isolation, the pocket of the beat). The grammar of each form defines correctness —
    there is no style-neutral "perfect" movement to which all styles approximate. Take
    the most examinable example: ballet's <em>turnout</em> is the rotation of each leg
    outward from the hip, in the ball-and-socket joint, so the feet open toward
    opposite walls. The rotation is anatomical (the femur rotating in the acetabulum),
    and a realistic dancer achieves maybe 45 degrees per hip — the fabled 180-degree
    fifth position is a rare anatomical gift. The teaching that matters is therefore
    <em>where the rotation comes from</em>: forcing turnout from the knee and ankle —
    because the feet are placed flat and the knees must somehow point outward — twists
    the knee joint and rolls the arches inward, which is why the first weeks of ballet
    training are spent finding true hip rotation with the fewest degrees honestly
    available. Alignment and placement are the same discipline generalized: bones
    stacked over bones so that muscles create movement rather than hold position.
  </p>
  <p>
    The physical layer, in turn, is trained with the same specificity the technique
    demands. Turnout strength means the deep hip rotators that <em>hold</em> rotation
    while moving, not just display it in first position; jumping requires elastic
    calf and quadriceps power trained by — jumping, in measured sets, plus the
    strength work that protects the landings; contemporary floorwork demands shoulder
    and upper-back strength that ballet never asks for, which is why dancers who move
    between styles retrain like athletes changing sports. The general principle is
    that every style's aesthetic secretly encodes a physical demand, and the
    knowledgeable dancer trains the demand, not just the look: the "line" is a
    flexibility-and-strength outcome, the "hang" of a released torso is back
    strength, the silent landing is deceleration power. Fitness for dance is not
    generic gym fitness; it is the chassis built to the style's own blueprint.
  </p>

  <h2>Expressive skills</h2>
  <p>
    Expressive skills are what the audience actually watches, and the syllabus names
    them precisely because they are trainable, not mysterious. <em>Focus</em>: where
    the eyes are is where the attention is, and audiences read gaze before gesture —
    a dancer looking down reads as doubt, a held focus to the back of the house as
    command. <em>Musicality</em>: the relationship between movement and music's
    rhythm, accent and texture — dancing <em>with</em> the beat, on it, behind it, or
    deliberately against it. <em>Phrasing</em>: the shaping of a movement sentence —
    where it breathes, where it suspends, where it releases — the dance equivalent of
    a musician's rubato. <em>Projection</em> and <em>commitment</em>: playing to the
    last row without inflating, and finishing movements fully — the audience's eye
    catches the dancer who abandons a phrase in its last ten percent. And
    <em>sensitivity to others</em>: in ensemble work, the felt timing between dancers
    that makes a group breathe as one organism.
  </p>
  <p>
    A simple experiment proves the layer exists independently of technique. Take eight
    counts anyone can walk. Danced on the beat, with level focus and even weight, it is
    transit. Danced with a suspension before beat three and a sudden release into it
    — with the eyes lifting as the weight goes — it is hope, or escape, or ambush. The
    movement vocabulary is identical; the expressive skills have been switched on.
    Movement analysts give these qualities a grammar of their own (Laban's "effort"
    qualities — the light sustained float against the strong sudden punch — naming
    how weight, time, space and flow combine), which is the analytic backbone of
    choreography and of dance criticism alike: performance is technique plus
    <em>chosen qualities</em>, chosen on purpose, phrase by phrase.
  </p>
  <p>
    Studied repertoire shows the layers combining in real work, which is why syllabuses
    set professional pieces for analysis. Wayne McGregor's <em>Infra</em> builds its
    meaning from pace and pattern — dancers in tight, flowing contemporary lines beneath
    a light-show of walking figures — so the expressive skill is control of ensemble
    texture at speed. Kenrick Sandy's <em>Emancipation of Expressionism</em> brings
    hip-hop grammar (popping, locking, krump) into a formal structure, and its
    expressiveness rides on isolation and groove — technique that <em>is</em>
    musicality. Lucy Bennett's <em>Artificial Things</em>, made with disabled and
    non-disabled dancers of Stopgap Dance Company, makes the point most sharply: its
    expressiveness lives in weight, touch and incline — qualities no syllabus reserves
    for any one body. Watching (or performing) set works with the five layers in mind
    turns admiration into analysis: the audience's felt response can be traced, almost
    always, to a technical and expressive choice a dancer made on purpose.
  </p>

  <h2>Safe dance practice and anatomy</h2>
  <p>
    Dance injuries cluster where anatomy is ignored, and safe dance practice is the
    applied counter-knowledge. <em>Warm-up</em> is physiology, not ritual: gradual
    aerobic work raises muscle temperature (warmer muscle is more elastic and contracts
    faster), mobilises the joints about to work, and rehearses the exact movement
    patterns of the class or piece — a warm-up should prepare the tissues about to
    work, which is why "stretching cold" is the classic beginner error. <em>Progressive
    loading</em> is how tissues adapt: muscle, tendon and bone all strengthen in
    response to manageable overload and weaken under sudden spikes — which is why
    jump counts rise gradually and why returning from a break at full intensity is how
    careers get interrupted. <em>Recovery and rest</em> are part of training, not
    exemptions from it: adaptation happens between loads. Landing mechanics matter
    quantifiably — the impact of a jump lands at several times body weight through
    ankle, knee and hip, and the technique that decelerates it (toes through feet,
    bending through the joints, silent landings) is the engineered answer.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> pain is progress, and flexibility is the point.
    Training discomfort and injury pain are different signals, and the professional
    convention is that sharp, localised, or persistent pain is a stop signal, not a
    badge. Flexibility pursued without the strength to control it produces hypermobile
    joints that fail — the goal is <em>usable</em> range, strength throughout it. And
    dance culture's most dangerous inherited belief — that lighter is always better —
    is now directly countered by sports nutrition: under-fuelled bodies break (stress
    fractures, halted hormonal function; the clinical spectrum is called RED-S, and
    dance medicine treats it as a first-semester topic, not a scandal discovered late).
  </p>
  <p>
    Injury management completes the applied-anatomy loop. The modern protocol has
    shifted from RICE to <em>POLICE</em> — protection, optimal loading, ice,
    compression, elevation — reflecting the finding that complete rest weakens tissue
    while graded, careful loading rebuilds it. Dancers and their teachers learn the
    difference between soreness, strain and something structural, and when to refer —
    because the difference between a week's modification and a season lost is usually
    the first forty-eight hours of honest management.
  </p>

  <h2>Rehearsal is where performance is built</h2>
  <p>
    The stage moment is the visible one percent: performance is assembled in rehearsal
    through repetition with intention. Rehearsal discipline mirrors the skill layers:
    early sessions are about <em>what</em> (counts, spacing, pathways — the technical
    map, much of it marked at half energy to conserve the body), middle sessions about
    <em>how</em> (dynamics, phrasing, the expressive decisions, now danced full-out in
    runs that build the stamina and the transitions fragments never show), and final
    runs about <em>who</em> — the communal timing and stagecraft that make eight
    individuals one event. Costume, lighting and spacing changes are rehearsed because
    every one of them changes what the body does: a bright light rewrites focus, a
    longer skirt rewrites line. By the time the audience arrives, the "natural" quality
    they saw was engineered — which is the highest compliment the craft receives.
    Technique fills the body; rehearsal teaches it to speak.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The layer that assembles everything is rehearsal, and it has its own science.
    <em>Marking</em> — running choreography at half energy, skipping lifts — conserves
    the body while consolidating memory; <em>full runs</em> build the stamina and the
    transitions that fragments never show; and motor-learning research distinguishes
    blocked practice (repeat one phrase until perfect) from varied, spaced practice,
    which produces the more durable, performance-ready memory. <em>Mental
    rehearsal</em> — visualising the phrase with full sensory detail — measurably
    aids consolidation, and dancers use it to learn without loading tissue. On stage,
    the trained skills are psychological: arousal control (the racing heart that ruins
    fine control, tamed by breath and routine), attention anchors for the
    blank-mind moment, and the commitment that reads as presence. Most professional
    dancers are injured in any given year — the epidemiology puts dance among the
    most demanding athletic professions — which is why modern companies employ
    physiotherapists and strength coaches, and why the smartest dancers cross-train:
    the aesthetic grammar may be centuries old, but the engineering around it is
    current.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> AQA GCSE Dance 8236 specification; Quin, Rafferty &amp; Tomlinson, <em>Safe Dance Practice</em>; the AQA dance anthology set works.</p>
